WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.931
Three thermometers read the same room

2
00:00:05.931 --> 00:00:07.527
twenty, twenty one

3
00:00:07.527 --> 00:00:09.601
and twenty three degrees.

4
00:00:09.601 --> 00:00:12.074
But they are not equally good.

5
00:00:12.153 --> 00:00:14.068
Their noise has standard

6
00:00:14.148 --> 00:00:16.860
deviations one half, one, and two.

7
00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:19.259
The plain average is twenty

8
00:00:19.335 --> 00:00:21.005
one point three three.

9
00:00:21.081 --> 00:00:23.662
Should the cheap thermometer count

10
00:00:23.738 --> 00:00:25.560
as much as the good one?

11
00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:26.850
Pause and think.

12
00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.306
Measure each residual in

13
00:00:32.384 --> 00:00:34.182
its own units of noise

14
00:00:34.182 --> 00:00:36.762
as the Mahalanobis distance did.

15
00:00:36.762 --> 00:00:40.123
Minimise the sum of each residual squared

16
00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:41.999
over its sigma squared.

17
00:00:41.999 --> 00:00:44.578
The weight matrix W is diagonal

18
00:00:44.578 --> 00:00:47.471
with entries one over sigma squared

19
00:00:47.471 --> 00:00:50.910
a weight of four, then one, then a quarter.

20
00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:54.599
Setting the gradient to zero gives

21
00:00:54.698 --> 00:00:57.644
the weighted normal equations

22
00:00:57.644 --> 00:01:00.591
A transpose W A, times x hat

23
00:01:00.591 --> 00:01:02.948
equals A transpose W b.

24
00:01:02.948 --> 00:01:06.876
When W is the inverse noise covariance

25
00:01:06.876 --> 00:01:11.296
the estimate has covariance A transpose W A

26
00:01:11.296 --> 00:01:12.180
inverse.

27
00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.330
Here A is a column of ones.

28
00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:18.280
So x hat is four times twenty

29
00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.898
plus twenty one

30
00:01:19.898 --> 00:01:22.943
plus a quarter of twenty three

31
00:01:22.943 --> 00:01:26.846
divided by four plus one plus a quarter

32
00:01:26.846 --> 00:01:29.320
twenty point three three.

33
00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:31.665
Its variance is one over

34
00:01:31.748 --> 00:01:35.157
five point two five: zero point one nine.

35
00:01:35.157 --> 00:01:36.487
Precisions add

36
00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.064
and the combined reading beats

37
00:01:39.147 --> 00:01:41.308
even the best thermometer

38
00:01:41.308 --> 00:01:44.550
whose variance is zero point two five.

39
00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:46.625
A natural shortcut

40
00:01:46.703 --> 00:01:49.535
trust each thermometer in proportion

41
00:01:49.613 --> 00:01:50.872
to its accuracy

42
00:01:50.872 --> 00:01:53.153
with weights one over sigma.

43
00:01:53.153 --> 00:01:54.490
Is that as good?

44
00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:58.053
With weights two, one, and one half

45
00:01:58.053 --> 00:02:01.367
the estimate is twenty point seven one

46
00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:03.439
and its variance is zero

47
00:02:03.521 --> 00:02:05.179
point two four five

48
00:02:05.179 --> 00:02:07.830
worse than zero point one nine.

49
00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:10.735
Equal weights are worse still

50
00:02:10.735 --> 00:02:13.886
with variance zero point five eight.

51
00:02:13.886 --> 00:02:17.463
The squared residual is in squared units

52
00:02:17.548 --> 00:02:19.677
so its weight must be one

53
00:02:19.762 --> 00:02:21.380
over sigma squared.

54
00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.445
Now fit a line.

55
00:02:23.445 --> 00:02:27.505
A sensor is calibrated at five settings

56
00:02:27.505 --> 00:02:30.971
zero to four, and its noise grows

57
00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:33.645
from zero point two at the

58
00:02:33.744 --> 00:02:36.120
start to two at the end.

59
00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:38.878
Ordinary least squares is pulled

60
00:02:38.953 --> 00:02:41.136
up by the noisy last reading

61
00:02:41.136 --> 00:02:43.468
intercept zero point six four

62
00:02:43.468 --> 00:02:45.500
slope two point three two.

63
00:02:45.575 --> 00:02:47.683
The weighted fit follows the

64
00:02:47.758 --> 00:02:50.994
precise early readings: one point zero two

65
00:02:50.994 --> 00:02:52.950
and one point nine three.

66
00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.070
The columns of A are all ones

67
00:02:56.166 --> 00:02:57.981
and the settings t.

68
00:02:57.981 --> 00:03:02.089
With the weights, the normal equations are

69
00:03:02.184 --> 00:03:04.095
a two by two system

70
00:03:04.095 --> 00:03:07.820
and its solution is the weighted line.

71
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.462
A good solver does not form A transpose W A.

72
00:03:12.462 --> 00:03:16.617
It divides each row of A and b by its sigma

73
00:03:16.617 --> 00:03:19.295
which makes the noise white

74
00:03:19.295 --> 00:03:23.080
and then it runs ordinary least squares

75
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:24.280
through Q R.

76
00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:26.413
Both fits are unbiased

77
00:03:26.413 --> 00:03:29.203
on average they find the true line.

78
00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.535
But the slope's standard deviation is zero

79
00:03:32.613 --> 00:03:35.403
point four one five without weights

80
00:03:35.403 --> 00:03:38.580
and zero point one eight five with them

81
00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.750
more than twice as precise.

82
00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.545
Why one over sigma squared?

83
00:03:43.545 --> 00:03:45.186
For Gaussian noise

84
00:03:45.268 --> 00:03:48.714
the log of the probability of the readings

85
00:03:48.796 --> 00:03:51.585
is minus one half of the residual

86
00:03:51.585 --> 00:03:54.949
times Sigma inverse, times the residual.

87
00:03:55.031 --> 00:03:57.903
So the most likely x is exactly the

88
00:03:57.985 --> 00:04:00.610
weighted least squares solution.

89
00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.783
One quadratic form does four jobs

90
00:04:03.783 --> 00:04:06.191
the exponent of the density

91
00:04:06.191 --> 00:04:08.018
the squared distance

92
00:04:08.018 --> 00:04:10.343
the length after whitening

93
00:04:10.343 --> 00:04:13.664
and the loss of weighted least squares.

94
00:04:13.664 --> 00:04:17.400
Its matrix is always the inverse covariance.

95
00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:20.475
Machine learning uses the same idea.

96
00:04:20.551 --> 00:04:22.821
A model can predict a mean and

97
00:04:22.897 --> 00:04:24.865
a variance for each input

98
00:04:24.941 --> 00:04:27.665
and minimise one half of the squared

99
00:04:27.741 --> 00:04:29.558
error over the variance

100
00:04:29.558 --> 00:04:31.828
plus the log of the variance

101
00:04:31.828 --> 00:04:33.645
weighted least squares

102
00:04:33.645 --> 00:04:35.310
with learned weights.

103
00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:38.308
Weight each measurement by

104
00:04:38.401 --> 00:04:40.446
one over its variance.

105
00:04:40.539 --> 00:04:43.235
The weighted normal equations

106
00:04:43.328 --> 00:04:45.839
are A transpose W A, x hat

107
00:04:45.839 --> 00:04:48.070
equals A transpose W b.

108
00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:49.558
Precisions add

109
00:04:49.651 --> 00:04:53.370
and dividing the rows by sigma turns the

110
00:04:53.463 --> 00:04:56.810
problem into ordinary least squares.

111
00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:00.788
The inverse covariance is the right matrix

112
00:05:00.788 --> 00:05:01.800
every time.
